Good afternoon to those who are on the east
coast.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:23.420
I'm Michele Dunn at the National Institute
of Health.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:29.980
I'd like to welcome you to this second talk
[INAUDIBLE] of the BD2K Guide to the Fundamentals

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.640
of Data Science.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:39.360
Now last week and for the next few weeks,
we're about computing.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.940
So last week's talk [INAUDIBLE], who gave
an overview of the area.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:53.800
This week, we'll have a narrower topic, but
still at an overview level, a high level 10,000

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:58.600
foot view of the specific topic of workflows
and pipelines.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:05.230
So we'll hear about what scientific work workflows
and pipelines are and how they will be useful

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:09.400
for even critical to science and big data
management.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:15.380
Our guest lecturer for today is Dr. Rommie
Amaro, who is a professor of chemistry and

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:19.330
biochemistry at the University of California,
San Diego.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:25.770
Her research is in computational methods for
drug discovery, where at San Diego, she oversees

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:32.040
a lab that does large scale simulations of
increasingly realistic biological systems

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:34.170
in order to investigate protein function.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:44.470
She has received many awards, including the
NIH New Innovator Award, as well as a very

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:45.490
prestigious PECASE award.

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:54.910
PECASE stands for the Presidential Early Career
Award for Scientists and Engineers.

00:01:54.910 --> 00:02:03.240
Dr. Amaro has received many NHI grants, is
the director or co-director of two very large

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.510
NIH awards.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:10.810
One is for the National Biomedical Computation
Resource, and another is for the Drug Design

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:11.880
Discovery Resource.

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:21.530
In addition, she has received a BD2K R25 award
to build an open educational resource, which

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:32.970
is a couple of MOOCs, as well as a way of
discovering different materials.

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:38.110
So to find out more about that, you can go
to Rommie's website.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:42.380
You can also go to the Big Data U website
as well.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:49.490
So please join me in welcoming Dr. Rommie
Amaro for this lecture today.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:50.490
Rommie.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.560
Thank you, Michelle.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:59.940
Yes, so I'm very happy to give this lecture
in the BD2K series.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:06.650
I'm going to be introducing a topic that is
sort of close to my heart as a biomedical

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:14.650
researcher, and that is the topic of workflows
and pipelines in biomedical research.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:20.640
So just to recap, you just heard an introduction
from Michelle, but I'm a professor of chemistry

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.650
and biochemistry at the University of California,
San Diego.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:30.069
I direct an NIHP41 multi-skilled modeling
resource called the National Biomedical Computation

00:03:30.069 --> 00:03:31.069
Resource.

00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:33.510
I'll tell you a little bit today about some
of the activities there.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:39.520
I'm also the codirector of the Drug Design
Data Resource, which is another NIH drug design

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.319
methods development, communityoriented resource.

00:03:43.319 --> 00:03:47.349
And I think the main thing that I want to
get across in this introduction slide is that

00:03:47.349 --> 00:03:55.400
I am a domain scientist, and as
you can tell from my background, I do work,

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:56.800
obviously, in data science.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:02.520
And there's many very important aspects that
affect what we do.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:09.379
I'm also a workflow convert, and so what I
want to do in today's seminar is-- because

00:04:09.379 --> 00:04:15.310
my understanding is we have a pretty broad
audience in terms of attendees.

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:20.411
So I've tried to make this a pretty general
talk all about workflows and pipelines, so

00:04:20.411 --> 00:04:24.340
I'm going to give a broad introduction to
workflows and pipelines.

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:29.790
I'm going to explain why they're both useful
and critical to what many of us are trying

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:32.960
to do in the biomedical research enterprise.

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:40.160
I'll showcase by way of a specific example
in biomedical research how workflows can be

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:46.520
useful for what we're doing and for enabling
reproducible results.

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:52.240
And I will also introduce an example workflow
tool that we've developed at one of my resources,

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:57.100
as well as discuss training opportunities
and additional material for more learning

00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:03.850
beyond just the introductory concepts that
I'll speak to today.

00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:09.470
So I think probably most everyone knows--
I'm sure on this call-- that it's an extremely

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:16.300
exciting time for computing in biomedical
research, and that's really for a number of

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:17.300
reasons.

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:21.390
One is, of course, that we've had a tremendous
increase in computing power.

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:27.010
And so what I'm showing here is just the increase
in compute power sort of as a function of

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:28.010
time.

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:31.120
And that's been available on the national
supercomputing resources.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:37.229
So the supercomputing resources are these
large architectures that are available for

00:05:37.229 --> 00:05:43.880
academic researchers in the United States
to perform various types of high performance

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:45.850
computing applications.

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:51.850
And biomedical research has certainly benefited
significantly from these resources.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:56.110
So this is just showing how in the early '90s,
these first computing resources came online.

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:59.960
They had about 12 CPUs, and about every four
to five years since then, we've increased

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:06.720
our computing power by an order of magnitude
taking us to 2015-- 2016-- 2017 now actually,

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:18.759
where we are really right now in what we call
the sustained petaflop performance capabilities.

00:06:18.759 --> 00:06:25.830
And that's brought to us by machines like
Titan at Oak Ridge, also Blue Waters at NCSA.

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:30.100
And something very critically important has
been actually the rise in graphical processing

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:35.460
unit technologies, or GPUs, which have really
brought supercomputing to the desktop.

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:40.380
And so a lot of the algorithms that we use
in biomedical computing research actually

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:47.960
port very well to the same architecture used
to develop great graphics for video games.

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:54.120
And so this has really also been a game changer
in a number of ways for biomedical computing.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:59.940
And then, of course, I think-- I'm sure others
in the series have mentioned that there was

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:06.690
recently-- they've initiated the National
Strategic Computing Initiative, or the NSCI,

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:11.830
in order to bring exascale computing into
reality, hopefully within the next five years

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:12.830
or so.

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:17.030
So just a tremendous increase in computing,
and that's really infiltrated many, many different

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:22.190
aspects, of course not only in biomedical
research, but for the scientific enterprise

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:23.350
generally speaking.

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:30.550
And, of course, it's not just computing, but
it's also data and access to a number of different

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:39.130
types of data, everything from proteomics
and genomics to increasing, amazing increasing

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:47.160
data availability for structural biology at
numerous resolutions, including things from

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:53.430
the structural data for molecular componentry
like in the PDB, as well as macromolecular

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:59.639
complexes thanks to advances in direct detectors
with cryo-electron microscopy and so forth.

00:07:59.639 --> 00:08:06.680
And so in terms of what we can do in biomedical
research, trying to bring together all these

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:14.530
different data streams to create new models--
for example, either structural models that

00:08:14.530 --> 00:08:23.340
might integrate proteomics, genomics, high
resolution structural biology details as well

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:28.210
as cellular substructure determined with electron
microscopy to bring together to make a multi-modal

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:30.500
multi-scale model.

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:36.860
There's many, many examples of how data is
infiltrating the research enterprise.

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:41.290
And so together, both computing and data are
really transforming how we do research.

00:08:41.290 --> 00:08:45.990
And so when we think about how things used
to be, I mean we're not just necessarily doing

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:47.670
one experiment at the bench.

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:54.730
I mean when we're posing a problem or a question
in biomedical research, it's much more than

00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:55.870
just one step.

00:08:55.870 --> 00:09:06.610
A typical or a state of the art example would
be starting with electron microscopy and all

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:13.420
the terrific advances there in terms of capabilities
and resolution-- for example, with [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:14.420
complexes.

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:15.450
So we start with our data.

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:18.040
We collect the data at the scope.

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:25.920
We then send that data-- for example, at UC
San Diego on a high speed network over to

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:27.170
a supercomputer.

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:32.450
And I'm just using this as an example here
at San Diego, where we can take the data directly

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:38.250
from the microscopes to the Comet supercomputer
in order to do image processing.

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:42.970
And then once we have that data, we bring--
it's ported again through a high speed network

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:45.550
from the supercomputer into the lab.

00:09:45.550 --> 00:09:52.240
And in the lab, we can build and interact
with these data sets using a variety of software,

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:56.490
resources, and develop models and interrogate
them.

00:09:56.490 --> 00:09:59.501
We might add additional components to those
models.

00:09:59.501 --> 00:10:06.940
For example, from a number of different community
data resources, just there's many different

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:11.570
resources to possibly pull from as you, again,
build and interrogate your model.

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:21.560
And then we might send that new sort of representation
of the data or the method-- we might send

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:24.940
back again to the supercomputers-- for example,
to Blue Waters.

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:30.500
And then we have more data that we process
and return back and can interact with in various

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:31.500
ways.

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:36.480
So this is showing sort of a possibility of
looking at the structures with virtual reality

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:41.050
or other-- sort of more than just three dimensions.

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:45.200
So really where we're at now is how where
experiments, simulation, networking, and expertise

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:47.680
are all combined into a super facility.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:51.420
But it's really sort of transforming how we
can do biomedical research.

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:56.480
And so the challenge or one major challenge,
when you think about sort of the complicated

00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:02.370
end to end scientific discovery process, a
major challenge is the growing complexity

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:07.550
of the computation and data that are required
to solve these highly

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:12.500
specialized significant problems in biomedical
research.

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:23.540
And this complex scientific enterprise really
brings a number of challenges, but one solution

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:29.380
to the growing complexity is the creation
of pipelines and workflows that are able to

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:35.720
assist in the assembly of datasets, as well
as integrate what we call domain-specific

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:41.649
software codes that are able to operate or
execute on various hardware systems or computing

00:11:41.649 --> 00:11:49.269
architectures and to do so in a way that is
robust, usable, and reproducible.

00:11:49.269 --> 00:11:53.399
And this is really where-- this is really
what's going to be demanded for or what is

00:11:53.399 --> 00:11:58.440
currently demanded and will continue to be
demanded for successful biomedical research

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:01.010
projects.

00:12:01.010 --> 00:12:03.420
So what is a scientific workflow?

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:09.519
So scientific workflows emerged as an answer
to the need to combine multiple data and computing

00:12:09.519 --> 00:12:12.839
components in an automated fashion.

00:12:12.839 --> 00:12:18.930
So scientific workflow is really basically
just a series of different computational steps

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:24.180
that scientists use to generate results.

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:31.899
And this may involve accessing multiple different
applications, scientific applications in different

00:12:31.899 --> 00:12:32.899
databases, as I mentioned.

00:12:32.899 --> 00:12:35.600
Also, it could involve a processing of data.

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:41.370
Most frequently it does using computationally
intensive jobs on various types of computing

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:45.220
platforms, and I'll tell you more about those
with some specific examples.

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:50.870
So the idea is just that it's capturing--
the scientific workflows are really capturing,

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:58.930
again, sort of the complicated many, many
step, end to end experiment, ideally, that

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:05.620
represents a scientific result.

00:13:05.620 --> 00:13:10.519
And so even backing off, sort of getting more
simple about it, so really what is a workflow?

00:13:10.519 --> 00:13:15.240
And so there's workflows everywhere-- of course,
not just in biomedical research.

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:20.490
So if we just think about when we're trying
to cook something, so I think one analogy

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:25.360
that I sort of like when I'm trying to introduce
the concept of workflows in pipelines to be

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:32.170
very introductory about it is to think about
how one cooks or how one basically cooks.

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:36.410
So when you're trying to create a recipe and
cook food, you have to basically-there's many

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:37.410
steps that you're doing.

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:38.410
You're shopping.

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:39.410
You're preparing the food.

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:42.451
You're cooking it, and you're enjoying it,
eating it, and you're also storing it.

00:13:42.451 --> 00:13:48.720
And so each of these steps takes a certain
amount of time, and it's essentially a workflow.

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:53.870
Some of those tasks that have to be performed
might be enjoyable, as sort of is illustrated

00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:57.209
here with the beige text.

00:13:57.209 --> 00:14:00.700
Some of them might be more tedious or chore-like.

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:06.240
And so one example is like we're making pasta
this evening, so there's all these different

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:08.610
things that we have to do.

00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:10.880
It's starting with the shopping for ingredients.

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:13.730
You can see like the raw ingredients on the
left.

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:17.300
You then have to prepare the different ingredients
maybe by chopping and dicing, et cetera.

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:20.620
You then have to cook it, and there might
be multiple things that you need to cook.

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:24.860
And then ultimately, you can serve it, eat
it, and then store it.

00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:32.329
So these are all different steps that represent
not a biomedical research workflow-- I'll

00:14:32.329 --> 00:14:35.320
get to that-- but just sort of the overall
concept.

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:41.019
And so each of these steps, they take place
in a certain order, and they have a certain

00:14:41.019 --> 00:14:42.560
time requirement.

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:45.649
And some of these things need to happen at
the same time as others.

00:14:45.649 --> 00:14:47.970
Some of them need to be staged.

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:49.579
Some things can happen in parallel.

00:14:49.579 --> 00:14:51.290
Others need to happen in series.

00:14:51.290 --> 00:14:55.230
So this is an example that we're all familiar
with.

00:14:55.230 --> 00:15:02.300
So we can think about workflows in this context,
even for biomedical research.

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:11.540
And so one thing that we might want-- one
way that workflows can be applicable here

00:15:11.540 --> 00:15:13.920
is if you want to speed things up.

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:19.260
So if you're cooking, for example, there are
things that time management can become very

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:24.180
important if you want to actually come up
with a result or a food, a cooked product

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:25.470
at a particular time.

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:30.860
So time management is important, and you can
take advantage of this downtime when you're

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:34.270
working on one element of the recipe.

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:38.949
You can then shift your attention to another
while something-- while, for example, the

00:15:38.949 --> 00:15:40.970
pasta is boiling.

00:15:40.970 --> 00:15:46.699
And so these recipes can be modified, and
they can be optimized.

00:15:46.699 --> 00:15:51.089
And also, similarly, analogously, what if
you have more than one cook?

00:15:51.089 --> 00:15:58.140
So can particular jobs be farmed out to different
people, or in this case of automated workflows,

00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:01.019
it's to different hardware architectures?

00:16:01.019 --> 00:16:07.050
And what can be done at different times or
the same times with multiple participants?

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:12.740
And so one example, again, going back to cooking,
so when we're just starting this, let's say

00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:14.949
that we have our vegetables.

00:16:14.949 --> 00:16:18.960
And this is something, so we can sort these
vegetables.

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:25.970
And if we have multiple cooks, each cook can
basically be chopping and doing the work on

00:16:25.970 --> 00:16:29.399
a certain set of the vegetables.

00:16:29.399 --> 00:16:35.740
And then now this would be equivalent to a
map function, where the input are the veggies,

00:16:35.740 --> 00:16:38.760
and the chopping is what we would call a userdefined
function.

00:16:38.760 --> 00:16:42.639
And the output of this operation would be
chopped groups of each kind of veggies.

00:16:42.639 --> 00:16:45.700
This is sort of the food equivalent to the
map function.

00:16:45.700 --> 00:16:50.540
Now we have these outputs of these different
piles of veggies.

00:16:50.540 --> 00:16:53.149
And we can think about, now we want to bring
them together.

00:16:53.149 --> 00:16:54.350
So we have all these different piles.

00:16:54.350 --> 00:16:55.350
We want to bring them together.

00:16:55.350 --> 00:16:59.260
We bring the tomatoes together, the peppers
together, the onions together.

00:16:59.260 --> 00:17:02.519
This is like similar to what we would call
a reduce step.

00:17:02.519 --> 00:17:08.730
So here now, the input is having chopped batches
of vegetables for each type, and the equivalent

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:14.189
user-defined function would be to combine
these vegetables basically based on type.

00:17:14.189 --> 00:17:19.649
So we can sort them, and then the output is
a bowl of vegetables per veggie kind.

00:17:19.649 --> 00:17:31.789
So we can-- again, these are sort of the food
analogies of developing functions and how

00:17:31.789 --> 00:17:38.659
you can sort of parse out different jobs to
different people in order to expedite or optimize

00:17:38.659 --> 00:17:39.659
what we're doing.

00:17:39.659 --> 00:17:44.479
And then we think about when you want make
something more elaborate, not just doing pasta.

00:17:44.479 --> 00:17:46.609
We want to make a Thanksgiving dinner.

00:17:46.609 --> 00:17:48.259
We have many, many dishes to make.

00:17:48.259 --> 00:17:50.809
We may have many helping participants.

00:17:50.809 --> 00:17:58.190
Each recipe and so forth has different preparation
time, cooking time, cooling time, et cetera.

00:17:58.190 --> 00:18:02.929
So as a chef or as somebody who's organizing
this, you have to think about all these things.

00:18:02.929 --> 00:18:08.809
Once you start cooking, what order do you
cook, et cetera, et cetera?

00:18:08.809 --> 00:18:12.690
And so this is an example of how we already
use workflow and are really familiar with

00:18:12.690 --> 00:18:17.470
them in sort of our just daily life.

00:18:17.470 --> 00:18:22.249
And now I'm going to give you some more concrete
examples of how these workflows can be useful,

00:18:22.249 --> 00:18:30.419
how workflows can also be useful in science.

00:18:30.419 --> 00:18:33.759
So workflows can take a number of different
forms.

00:18:33.759 --> 00:18:44.210
They can be, for example, very simple pipelines,
where one example is that you have data on

00:18:44.210 --> 00:18:48.639
a normal disk, so you can read and choose
the file.

00:18:48.639 --> 00:18:55.549
You can then create a function that basically
does a multiple sequence alignment for you.

00:18:55.549 --> 00:18:59.659
And then it outputs that alignment into something
that does a phylogenetic analysis.

00:18:59.659 --> 00:19:01.860
And then you can output that out.

00:19:01.860 --> 00:19:07.820
It can transfer the output of the phylogenetic
analysis into something where you can actually

00:19:07.820 --> 00:19:12.899
visualize it with, for example, using trees
and so forth.

00:19:12.899 --> 00:19:18.549
Workflows can also integrate components to
analyze local data.

00:19:18.549 --> 00:19:25.639
So this is an example of a workflow drawn
in Kepler, which I'll tell you more about,

00:19:25.639 --> 00:19:29.220
for computational flow cytometry data analysis.

00:19:29.220 --> 00:19:34.220
So here, it starts to get a little more complicated.

00:19:34.220 --> 00:19:40.109
But basically, different components and different
data components can actually come together

00:19:40.109 --> 00:19:49.269
to participate in the analysis of data that's
held locally.

00:19:49.269 --> 00:19:55.200
We can also use pipelines and workflows to
integrate many different online tools and

00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:59.710
several different kinds of databases using
complex programming structures.

00:19:59.710 --> 00:20:07.229
So here's an example of a workflow that's
doing some micro array analysis to identify

00:20:07.229 --> 00:20:09.119
new candidate target chains.

00:20:09.119 --> 00:20:12.770
And here, we start with micro array data.

00:20:12.770 --> 00:20:14.929
We do a clustering.

00:20:14.929 --> 00:20:16.480
analysis.

00:20:16.480 --> 00:20:19.190
We can identify the genes.

00:20:19.190 --> 00:20:22.429
We can get them to the sequence retrieval.

00:20:22.429 --> 00:20:25.529
We send that back out to the NCBI class search.

00:20:25.529 --> 00:20:31.859
It brings back other genomic sequences, and
then we can look for things like promoters

00:20:31.859 --> 00:20:37.570
and promoter model generators in order to
generate a consensus sequence, which be put

00:20:37.570 --> 00:20:41.830
back onto the BLAST search and then, again,
to finally identify a new candidate target

00:20:41.830 --> 00:20:42.830
gene.

00:20:42.830 --> 00:20:48.659
So this is a more sort of elaborate example
of how workflows can be used to integrate

00:20:48.659 --> 00:20:53.529
a number of different online tools, as well
as online databases in order to help facilitate

00:20:53.529 --> 00:20:56.779
biomedical research.

00:20:56.779 --> 00:21:03.960
Workflows can also have concurrent analysis
and data management steps that actually are

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:07.309
distributed to different computing platforms.

00:21:07.309 --> 00:21:16.169
And so this is an example that I will speak
much more about when I give a concrete example.

00:21:16.169 --> 00:21:24.139
But here, we can actually-- what this workflow
is doing is actually one of the sort of what

00:21:24.139 --> 00:21:30.830
we call actors or modules in this workflow
is actually being used to distribute or to

00:21:30.830 --> 00:21:36.169
sort of spawn out many different jobs in parallel
on an HPC platform.

00:21:36.169 --> 00:21:40.609
And then the analysis on each of those can
come back asynchronously or synchronously

00:21:40.609 --> 00:21:45.700
in order to determine the results of the jobs.

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:50.029
Of course, workflows can also integrate cloud
resources.

00:21:50.029 --> 00:21:57.859
So they can tie into and connect to not just
local data, but data that can be in the cloud

00:21:57.859 --> 00:22:00.879
or other places.

00:22:00.879 --> 00:22:05.190
So overall, biomedical research workflows
allow us to assemble

00:22:05.190 --> 00:22:11.169
complex processes easily, transparently access
different computing resources, which is increasingly

00:22:11.169 --> 00:22:16.470
important as computing enters more and more
elements of biomedical research enterprise.

00:22:16.470 --> 00:22:24.359
It allows us to automate it in more rigorous
fashion, incorporate multiple software tools,

00:22:24.359 --> 00:22:29.309
and I'll talk more about also why that's so
important, again, in the example I'll show

00:22:29.309 --> 00:22:30.379
you.

00:22:30.379 --> 00:22:36.139
They also help enable reproducibility, which,
of course, we all know is a major challenge

00:22:36.139 --> 00:22:43.970
for science in general, but also for biomedical
research-- how we can ensure that when we

00:22:43.970 --> 00:22:50.140
have one student or multiple students working
on a project, that then when those students

00:22:50.140 --> 00:22:55.309
leave, and new ones come in, that the protocols
are being followed correctly and that we really

00:22:55.309 --> 00:23:01.309
knew how we get generated the model, but in
a very specific kind of way that it's really,

00:23:01.309 --> 00:23:02.389
truly useful.

00:23:02.389 --> 00:23:07.820
And of course, the best research workflows
will really build around a community development

00:23:07.820 --> 00:23:13.460
model, and this is something that I think
many fields are now-- many subfields within

00:23:13.460 --> 00:23:18.049
biomedical research are really embracing.

00:23:18.049 --> 00:23:24.590
And that's the idea that so I can develop
workflows in my lab and share them very easily

00:23:24.590 --> 00:23:30.479
with colleagues and with the community, and
we can use each other's efforts to build,

00:23:30.479 --> 00:23:41.740
again, and use these sort of more elaborate,
reproducible frameworks for science.

00:23:41.740 --> 00:23:47.840
And so there are many scientific workflow
systems, as one would imagine.

00:23:47.840 --> 00:23:52.610
This is something that people have been working
on for at least two decades.

00:23:52.610 --> 00:23:56.529
So one of the earliest scientific workflow
systems was something called DiscoveryNet.

00:23:56.529 --> 00:24:02.359
And then there were a whole bunch like Triana,
Pegasus, Kepler, Taverna, Pipeline Pilot coming

00:24:02.359 --> 00:24:05.479
on in the early 2000s.

00:24:05.479 --> 00:24:09.419
KNIME is another very popular workflow system.

00:24:09.419 --> 00:24:14.029
We have Vistrails, GenePattern, so you can
see sort of the evolution of these through

00:24:14.029 --> 00:24:16.330
about 2010.

00:24:16.330 --> 00:24:22.489
And what I am going to mostly talk about today
in the concrete examples is a workflow system

00:24:22.489 --> 00:24:23.499
called Kepler.

00:24:23.499 --> 00:24:27.499
This is, again, a very popular common workflow
framework.

00:24:27.499 --> 00:24:33.100
It has a diverse library of scientific components
and use cases.

00:24:33.100 --> 00:24:40.870
It allows transparent support for multiple
workflow engines, so you can actually connect.

00:24:40.870 --> 00:24:48.179
So even within a Kepler workflow, you can
actually call other workflow systems-- for

00:24:48.179 --> 00:24:52.830
example, like KNIME-- in order to execute
an end to end process.

00:24:52.830 --> 00:24:58.619
And it's used by many communities, not just
in biomedical research, but also in fire detection

00:24:58.619 --> 00:25:00.919
and all sorts of things.

00:25:00.919 --> 00:25:06.039
So it's been a useful community development
tool.

00:25:06.039 --> 00:25:09.809
So that's the one that I'll mostly focus on
today, but I just also mention, of course,

00:25:09.809 --> 00:25:15.969
that there are many different programs, and
a lot of them have strengths and weaknesses.

00:25:15.969 --> 00:25:19.740
I'm sure many other learning opportunities
if you want to dig in on the details of these

00:25:19.740 --> 00:25:22.960
other workflow systems.

00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:28.470
So how do I use workflows, and how did I come
to sort of seeing a light with how useful

00:25:28.470 --> 00:25:33.630
they can be and how important they actually
are to the scientific enterprise?

00:25:33.630 --> 00:25:37.159
So this example I'm going to tell you about
is one that's in my domain of expertise, which

00:25:37.159 --> 00:25:40.770
is computer-aided drug discovery.

00:25:40.770 --> 00:25:47.419
So the work that I do in my lab and that we
do in some of my resources actually involves

00:25:47.419 --> 00:25:53.990
the computational modeling of atomic level
systems in biomedicine.

00:25:53.990 --> 00:25:59.539
And so we'd like to think one of the major
approaches we use is something called molecular

00:25:59.539 --> 00:26:01.369
dynamic simulations.

00:26:01.369 --> 00:26:07.590
And I'm showing here a picture of a computational
microscope, so this was sort of a phrase coined

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:14.330
by Klau Schulten, which basically is sort
of we're trying to use these computers, the

00:26:14.330 --> 00:26:20.119
HPC computers mainly, but now actually, it's
evolved into using also GPU architectures.

00:26:20.119 --> 00:26:25.710
We're trying to use these methods to gain
additional insight into these molecular systems

00:26:25.710 --> 00:26:29.609
that we sometimes cannot access with experiments
alone.

00:26:29.609 --> 00:26:35.389
And so what these simulations involve, so
basically we're representing all the atoms

00:26:35.389 --> 00:26:36.950
of our system.

00:26:36.950 --> 00:26:40.159
We're representing them at the atomic level,
and so we define what we call a potential

00:26:40.159 --> 00:26:41.341
function, which you can see there.

00:26:41.341 --> 00:26:42.341
So every two atoms that are connected form
a bond.

00:26:42.341 --> 00:26:46.559
Every three atoms that are connected form
an angle.

00:26:46.559 --> 00:26:47.960
Every four atoms are in dihedrals and torsions.

00:26:47.960 --> 00:26:54.619
And each of these terms, each of these elements
of the system actually has a term in a potential

00:26:54.619 --> 00:26:55.619
function.

00:26:55.619 --> 00:26:57.219
And then we're basically using physics.

00:26:57.219 --> 00:27:02.419
We're just simply integrating it into the
equation of motion over time in order to build

00:27:02.419 --> 00:27:07.849
up a dynamical trajectory of the time-dependent
dynamics of our system.

00:27:07.849 --> 00:27:19.279
So we're basically representing these molecular
level systems numerically and trying to determine

00:27:19.279 --> 00:27:21.940
the wiggling and jiggling of the atoms, if
you will.

00:27:21.940 --> 00:27:28.719
And so these experiments use a number of different
community developed tools like NAMD, AMBER,

00:27:28.719 --> 00:27:30.549
many different tools, actually.

00:27:30.549 --> 00:27:36.710
And the engine that this microscope runs on
are basically the supercomputing platforms,

00:27:36.710 --> 00:27:40.519
and now graphical processing units, and also
sometimes local desktops and so forth.

00:27:40.519 --> 00:27:44.269
But they can run in many places.

00:27:44.269 --> 00:27:52.299
So one of the major goals that we had it in
my lab was to try to use simulation in early

00:27:52.299 --> 00:27:53.749
stage drug discovery.

00:27:53.749 --> 00:27:59.690
And what I'm showing on the let is and old
napkin sketch from, actually, when I was a

00:27:59.690 --> 00:28:00.730
post-doc.

00:28:00.730 --> 00:28:06.409
And we were just sort of like walking through
how we could use the output of the simulations,

00:28:06.409 --> 00:28:10.049
which as I mentioned, is just sort of the
wiggling and jiggling or the time-dependent

00:28:10.049 --> 00:28:16.570
dynamics of these molecules, how we could
use this in computeraided drug design.

00:28:16.570 --> 00:28:19.909
And so it's really-- you can see this sort
of like chicken scratch.

00:28:19.909 --> 00:28:26.539
And over the course of some years, we were
able to actually develop methods that would

00:28:26.539 --> 00:28:32.249
be gainfully used to incorporate simulation
in drug discovery.

00:28:32.249 --> 00:28:40.139
And so what that would mean is so you can
see that I'm advancing-- the concept here

00:28:40.139 --> 00:28:41.619
of the workflow is advancing.

00:28:41.619 --> 00:28:45.769
And this is sort of like I think a normal
way for-- this is a very sort of natural,

00:28:45.769 --> 00:28:49.089
normal way for scientific methods and projects
to evolve.

00:28:49.089 --> 00:28:54.080
First, you start with an idea that's literally
a sketch on the back of a napkin, and you

00:28:54.080 --> 00:28:55.080
work on it.

00:28:55.080 --> 00:28:57.450
You keep working
on it, and then you get the first prototype.

00:28:57.450 --> 00:29:00.129
And then you can continue to elaborate.

00:29:00.129 --> 00:29:07.390
And ultimately, what I'll show you many slides
down the road is that we've gone from basically

00:29:07.390 --> 00:29:14.119
the napkin-type drawing to really a hardened
workflow product.

00:29:14.119 --> 00:29:15.239
And so why do we care?

00:29:15.239 --> 00:29:20.219
So just in case there are some scientists
out there, I'm going to give you a little

00:29:20.219 --> 00:29:23.320
bit of science in addition to the computing.

00:29:23.320 --> 00:29:29.879
So one successful example of this involves
P53, which is the guardian of the genome.

00:29:29.879 --> 00:29:37.029
So P53 is a protein that's-- a transcription
factor that's mutated in over 50% of all human

00:29:37.029 --> 00:29:38.029
cancers.

00:29:38.029 --> 00:29:47.450
And so it's a molecule that senses-- basically,
when the cell has some type of damage like

00:29:47.450 --> 00:29:53.009
DNA breaks or UV irradiation, P53 should sense
that damage and then regulate pathways related

00:29:53.009 --> 00:29:58.600
to programmed cell death like growth of Ras
and apoptosis and prevention of [INAUDIBLE].

00:29:58.600 --> 00:30:03.279
And so what happens in over 60% of human cancers
is that P53 is mutated, and it's inactive.

00:30:03.279 --> 00:30:05.379
And so these tumors just proliferate.

00:30:05.379 --> 00:30:13.410
And so we were very interested in trying to
understand what was going on there, how the

00:30:13.410 --> 00:30:15.779
destabilization worked, et cetera.

00:30:15.779 --> 00:30:22.239
And a longstanding dream of cancer biologists,
actually, was to try to basically reactivate

00:30:22.239 --> 00:30:25.960
the cancerous or inactive P53 with small molecules.

00:30:25.960 --> 00:30:31.879
And so the idea would be that we would have
a cancer mutant that has this inactive P53.

00:30:31.879 --> 00:30:34.690
You would bind some drug to it, and you would
reactivate it.

00:30:34.690 --> 00:30:41.619
And then those cells that formerly were just
proliferating and metastasizing, that they

00:30:41.619 --> 00:30:45.789
would reengage their programmed cell death
and die.

00:30:45.789 --> 00:30:50.779
And so there were a number of compounds that
had been found over the years after about

00:30:50.779 --> 00:30:52.099
20 years of research.

00:30:52.099 --> 00:30:58.279
They had found a handful of compounds, but
they didn't know exactly how they worked.

00:30:58.279 --> 00:31:04.259
And so we began doing molecular dynamic simulations
of this target in order to try to figure out--

00:31:04.259 --> 00:31:09.539
again, we're interested in looking at the
dynamics, because we had known that actually

00:31:09.539 --> 00:31:11.039
it was quite a flexible protein.

00:31:11.039 --> 00:31:14.269
So this is the output of a molecular dynamic
simulation.

00:31:14.269 --> 00:31:16.269
You can see all the atoms wiggling and jiggling.

00:31:16.269 --> 00:31:22.179
And what I want to bring your attention to
is this area in the middle, where we have

00:31:22.179 --> 00:31:26.929
a-- I don't know if you can see my cursor.

00:31:26.929 --> 00:31:32.149
But there's a cysteine residue here in a pocket,
where you see a lot of mobility.

00:31:32.149 --> 00:31:35.879
And so what was really interesting from a
scientific perspective was that if you looked

00:31:35.879 --> 00:31:41.259
at the PDB database, there's over 100 different
structures of P53, and they all have the same

00:31:41.259 --> 00:31:44.620
sort of architecture around that particular
cysteine, which is actually-- it's sort of

00:31:44.620 --> 00:31:47.789
like tucked away in back, sort of a closed
pocket.

00:31:47.789 --> 00:31:54.080
And so what we see in the computational simulations
is actually the formation of an open pocket

00:31:54.080 --> 00:31:55.739
around this residue.

00:31:55.739 --> 00:32:01.159
And this is very intriguing, because we found
this new site, and we also saw that it might,

00:32:01.159 --> 00:32:07.749
perhaps, actually druggable, so we performed
small molecule docking into that site.

00:32:07.749 --> 00:32:16.289
And basically, using the work-- so here's
sort of just a sketch of the workflow for

00:32:16.289 --> 00:32:22.029
virtual screening, where we can take these
protein or receptor structures.

00:32:22.029 --> 00:32:26.059
And this could be coming from the PDB database
with x-ray crystallography.

00:32:26.059 --> 00:32:30.350
It could also be simulation
data from the molecular dynamic simulations.

00:32:30.350 --> 00:32:31.850
We have another database of ligands.

00:32:31.850 --> 00:32:34.919
In this example, we're using the NCI diversity
set.

00:32:34.919 --> 00:32:40.259
We bring them together using AutoDock Vina,
so this is another procedural operation.

00:32:40.259 --> 00:32:45.429
We then do what's called post-processing,
and then we pass our predictions to our experimental

00:32:45.429 --> 00:32:46.869
collaborators for testing.

00:32:46.869 --> 00:32:52.139
With this workflow, we were able to find one
reactivation molecule called stictic acid,

00:32:52.139 --> 00:32:56.989
and our collaborator showed that it there
was dose-dependant rescue in mammalian cancer

00:32:56.989 --> 00:32:57.989
cells.

00:32:57.989 --> 00:33:00.839
But one of the really interesting things also
was that in addition to finding a new compound,

00:33:00.839 --> 00:33:05.539
we were able to rationalize the mechanism
of action for one of the formerly discovered

00:33:05.539 --> 00:33:07.929
compounds that's actually in clinical trials.

00:33:07.929 --> 00:33:08.929
So this was really exciting.

00:33:08.929 --> 00:33:11.080
Then we wanted to scale it up.

00:33:11.080 --> 00:33:19.559
And by scaling up, we really wanted to be
able to screen against many, many more compounds,

00:33:19.559 --> 00:33:24.909
and also to do sort of much more sophisticated
post-processing with regard to ranking of

00:33:24.909 --> 00:33:26.009
compounds.

00:33:26.009 --> 00:33:31.139
And one way this was enabled was through workflows.

00:33:31.139 --> 00:33:39.969
And so with this sort of more automated pipeline--
so this I'll just call a pipeline, because

00:33:39.969 --> 00:33:44.600
it's really just showing sort of like the
procedural steps of what we're doing.

00:33:44.600 --> 00:33:51.230
We were able to discover many more compounds
in a very short amount of time-- basically

00:33:51.230 --> 00:33:55.989
in six months-- than in all the research efforts
of the previous 20 years combined.

00:33:55.989 --> 00:34:03.099
So once we were able to scale it, and with
a good model, the discovery really sort of

00:34:03.099 --> 00:34:04.549
took off.

00:34:04.549 --> 00:34:10.570
And so what we were able to do is really go
from what was just a chicken scratch pipe

00:34:10.570 --> 00:34:15.649
dream to sort of pipeline to now a scalable,
automated workflow.

00:34:15.649 --> 00:34:19.010
And so what does that mean?

00:34:19.010 --> 00:34:25.230
So when we were doing these experiments that
I was telling you about here, this is really--

00:34:25.230 --> 00:34:32.350
as many people in research will do and in
computational biomedical research I'm sure,

00:34:32.350 --> 00:34:35.889
but in many other fields also, again when
you're developing a prototype, very often

00:34:35.889 --> 00:34:40.379
what happens is you have a post-doc or a student,
who has a set of scripts.

00:34:40.379 --> 00:34:43.300
And they're running these scripts, and sometimes
these scripts can be quite elaborate.

00:34:43.300 --> 00:34:51.889
But there's many, many steps involved in this
workflow, everything from selection of the

00:34:51.889 --> 00:34:57.240
structures that you're inputting into MD to
the creation of the molecular dynamics model,

00:34:57.240 --> 00:35:02.240
running of the trajectories, selecting the
structures, how you process the ligands, how

00:35:02.240 --> 00:35:03.769
you're doing the docking.

00:35:03.769 --> 00:35:09.140
There are so many things that one really needs
to keep track.

00:35:09.140 --> 00:35:12.080
And we do our best.

00:35:12.080 --> 00:35:16.410
I think everybody-- all biomedical researchers
try to do their best in terms of keeping very

00:35:16.410 --> 00:35:21.779
careful lab notebooks, digital notebooks,
saving the scripts, and so on and so forth.

00:35:21.779 --> 00:35:29.140
And that gets us pretty far, but it still
has significant-- it still has significant

00:35:29.140 --> 00:35:30.140
limitations.

00:35:30.140 --> 00:35:34.839
And I'll talk about some of those limitations
and how workflows can help us get around those.

00:35:34.839 --> 00:35:43.470
So what we did was actually to develop-- using
the Kepler workflow system, we were able to

00:35:43.470 --> 00:35:46.380
develop a scalable, automated workflow.

00:35:46.380 --> 00:35:53.220
So we would basically have-- we would be able
to, in this one example, we would have all

00:35:53.220 --> 00:35:55.279
those small molecules that we wanted to screen
against.

00:35:55.279 --> 00:36:00.390
We put those in a directory, and this Kepler
workflow would see this directory.

00:36:00.390 --> 00:36:05.150
It would go into the contents, and then actually
perform a series of steps that were what we

00:36:05.150 --> 00:36:08.740
call parameterized ligands, which is getting
them ready for molecular dynamics.

00:36:08.740 --> 00:36:12.109
We can then simulate them and then do processing.

00:36:12.109 --> 00:36:17.010
And each one of these-- so this little box
here, this ligand parameterization, is actually

00:36:17.010 --> 00:36:25.890
represented by-- if you actually dig into
this one task, it's actually many subtasks.

00:36:25.890 --> 00:36:30.640
And this is really how it is for many different
steps in biomedical research.

00:36:30.640 --> 00:36:36.860
So when we take-- instead of using scripts
for this anti-chamber step and this Gaussian

00:36:36.860 --> 00:36:43.570
step and so forth, we can actually encode
it into a workflow, such that all we have

00:36:43.570 --> 00:36:45.640
to do is have a directory of ligands.

00:36:45.640 --> 00:36:47.380
And then everything else just flows.

00:36:47.380 --> 00:36:49.410
As the output finishes, it flows to the next.

00:36:49.410 --> 00:36:50.690
It flows to the next.

00:36:50.690 --> 00:36:52.220
And everything about it is tracked.

00:36:52.220 --> 00:36:54.049
Again, I'll tell you more about that.

00:36:54.049 --> 00:37:02.200
So all the software versions, all of like
when you actually ran the jobs, all the library

00:37:02.200 --> 00:37:05.010
dependencies, it can all be tracked through
something called provenance.

00:37:05.010 --> 00:37:13.599
And so when we recapitulate these very complex
end to end experiments into these automated

00:37:13.599 --> 00:37:20.650
workflows, it brings tremendous benefit, including
increased reuse, because it's much easier,

00:37:20.650 --> 00:37:28.190
generally speaking, for a new student or post-doc
to pick up and run these automated workflows

00:37:28.190 --> 00:37:33.680
if they don't have to stitch together a bunch
of loose scripts.

00:37:33.680 --> 00:37:37.859
It increases reproducibility, because we've
now really tracked everything and all of the

00:37:37.859 --> 00:37:39.930
dependencies with provenance.

00:37:39.930 --> 00:37:44.390
It helps us scale execution both of the problem
and the solution.

00:37:44.390 --> 00:37:47.109
So I'll talk a little bit more about scaling
of execution.

00:37:47.109 --> 00:37:53.230
So that just essentially means that we can
more easily connect to different computing

00:37:53.230 --> 00:37:57.420
resources in order to carry out the job, and
it makes it easy to compare methods.

00:37:57.420 --> 00:38:01.440
So if you just want to really change only
one thing, you can just go in and change one

00:38:01.440 --> 00:38:04.470
parameter inside one box and rerun it and
compare.

00:38:04.470 --> 00:38:09.000
And it also makes it significantly easier
to train students, because now they see, when

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:12.630
they sit down for the first time, they can
see the whole pipeline and dig in on each

00:38:12.630 --> 00:38:16.849
module in a way that is simplified.

00:38:16.849 --> 00:38:25.019
So just one of the steps that we carried out
in the very complicated end to end process

00:38:25.019 --> 00:38:30.220
of small molecule discovery, one of the processes,
as I mentioned, was just running molecular

00:38:30.220 --> 00:38:31.220
dynamic simulations.

00:38:31.220 --> 00:38:38.579
And so we've worked to develop, again, a workflow
tool using Kepler that just carries out AMBER,

00:38:38.579 --> 00:38:42.190
what we call AMBER molecular dynamics.

00:38:42.190 --> 00:38:46.310
So AMBER is an MD engine that's available
broadly to the community.

00:38:46.310 --> 00:38:53.859
And we've developed now a workflow tool that
just allows people to carry out research quality,

00:38:53.859 --> 00:39:00.980
molecular dynamic simulations using AMBER
GPU processing technology and the Kepler workflow

00:39:00.980 --> 00:39:01.980
system.

00:39:01.980 --> 00:39:05.970
And so these are, again, sort of a very high
level representation of different steps that

00:39:05.970 --> 00:39:09.960
we have to do in order to carry out molecular
dynamics.

00:39:09.960 --> 00:39:15.940
And so if we look in this one step of something
we call minimization, if you actually

00:39:15.940 --> 00:39:19.559
zoom into this minimization box, there's many
steps.

00:39:19.559 --> 00:39:21.710
It's not just one step for minimization.

00:39:21.710 --> 00:39:25.269
There's actually five different minimization
steps.

00:39:25.269 --> 00:39:31.240
And so what this tool does again is in an
automated fashion, sort of feed from-- as

00:39:31.240 --> 00:39:36.089
the first minimization step finishes, then
the output feeds into the second step and

00:39:36.089 --> 00:39:37.089
so on and so forth.

00:39:37.089 --> 00:39:41.569
And then the outputs from minimization go
to a stage we call heating, and then to equilibration.

00:39:41.569 --> 00:39:44.450
And again, in equilibration, you can see it's
multistage.

00:39:44.450 --> 00:39:48.039
And then when we get to production, one interesting
thing here is that you see that there's all

00:39:48.039 --> 00:39:53.599
these different little outputs, so it's going--
basically, when we go to production, instead

00:39:53.599 --> 00:39:59.869
of just running on one GPU, we actually scale
it out to multiple GPUs in parallel.

00:39:59.869 --> 00:40:09.619
And so this workflow tool basically allows
the researchers to do that very, very easily.

00:40:09.619 --> 00:40:15.010
And then so it also has sort of a graphical
user interface for users.

00:40:15.010 --> 00:40:23.079
And so again, sort of the molecular dynamics
workflow, you can actually-- users can go

00:40:23.079 --> 00:40:25.099
into each of these little boxes.

00:40:25.099 --> 00:40:32.099
And at the base level they can go in and edit
the key parameters.

00:40:32.099 --> 00:40:36.789
Different parameters can be exposed or hidden
depending on-- because some parameters, as

00:40:36.789 --> 00:40:40.440
people know in various programs, basically
maybe never change.

00:40:40.440 --> 00:40:44.260
Others, you have to change depending on your
system.

00:40:44.260 --> 00:40:51.509
So this can be configured, so we configured
it for molecular dynamics using sort of best

00:40:51.509 --> 00:40:57.529
practices for the community, but this can
be-- different levels of detail can be hidden

00:40:57.529 --> 00:41:01.559
or revealed depending on the application.

00:41:01.559 --> 00:41:09.450
And then we can, in terms of where the simulations
actually run, they have different options.

00:41:09.450 --> 00:41:11.670
You can have local execution options.

00:41:11.670 --> 00:41:17.930
You can run on a local cluster, or you can
also port out to, for example, the exceed

00:41:17.930 --> 00:41:21.240
computing resources like Gordon or Comet.

00:41:21.240 --> 00:41:29.490
And so 
the gray circle here is just sort of showing

00:41:29.490 --> 00:41:35.450
all the different types of heterogeneous computing
resources that these workflows can connect

00:41:35.450 --> 00:41:36.450
to.

00:41:36.450 --> 00:41:41.000
And that includes, for example, operating
in the Amazon cloud.

00:41:41.000 --> 00:41:47.859
And even within one workflow, you can have
many different computational architectures

00:41:47.859 --> 00:41:48.859
engaged.

00:41:48.859 --> 00:41:52.660
That's one of the advantages of Kepler is
that if I have one step that needs to run

00:41:52.660 --> 00:41:54.630
on my local GPU, it can just do that.

00:41:54.630 --> 00:42:01.490
But it can also route to NSF resources or,
again, the cloud or any sort of computing

00:42:01.490 --> 00:42:02.490
architecture.

00:42:02.490 --> 00:42:09.280
So we can have multiple architectures engaged
within one single workflow.

00:42:09.280 --> 00:42:10.809
OK.

00:42:10.809 --> 00:42:15.329
So we're getting to the-- we're 40 minutes
in.

00:42:15.329 --> 00:42:19.319
So how does one even start conceptualizing
a computational workflow?

00:42:19.319 --> 00:42:23.869
Well, in general, we would advice to start
with the workflow as a black box.

00:42:23.869 --> 00:42:27.960
And so just treat the whole workflow as a
black box, and you'd have to think of, what

00:42:27.960 --> 00:42:30.700
is the use case and application?

00:42:30.700 --> 00:42:34.250
So what is the real science question that
this workflow is solving?

00:42:34.250 --> 00:42:38.900
So you're going to have here some input data,
and then you're going to have some process.

00:42:38.900 --> 00:42:45.280
And this is just shown as one giant black
box, but it's sometimes many, many steps.

00:42:45.280 --> 00:42:49.200
And then you have to think about what your
output is going to be.

00:42:49.200 --> 00:42:50.349
And then you have to conceptualize.

00:42:50.349 --> 00:42:55.280
Then you really need to dig into, well, what
are my steps?

00:42:55.280 --> 00:43:01.240
So in terms of cooking, this is things like
if I'm working on a pie, I have to put the

00:43:01.240 --> 00:43:05.829
ingredients together, cook and chill it, et
cetera, bake turkey, make the side dishes.

00:43:05.829 --> 00:43:09.880
So this would be for Thanksgiving dinner all
the things you need to do.

00:43:09.880 --> 00:43:16.559
For a scientific application, this might be
things like you've got to collect the data,

00:43:16.559 --> 00:43:17.559
the [INAUDIBLE] data.

00:43:17.559 --> 00:43:20.500
You have to do the sequence alignment, et
cetera.

00:43:20.500 --> 00:43:25.589
And so you basically treat each step like
a workflow, like a mini workflow.

00:43:25.589 --> 00:43:30.240
So you want to basically try to reach what
we would call, I guess, an atomic functional

00:43:30.240 --> 00:43:33.240
step.

00:43:33.240 --> 00:43:37.220
So you really-- when you think about all these
things, you have to find the data, access

00:43:37.220 --> 00:43:38.220
the data.

00:43:38.220 --> 00:43:42.500
Maybe you have to move the data, and then
once you have the data, it has to be cleaned,

00:43:42.500 --> 00:43:43.500
possibly integrated.

00:43:43.500 --> 00:43:46.509
Maybe you need to divide it up or pre-process
it.

00:43:46.509 --> 00:43:52.240
And then you get into actually running the
jobs if you have like, for example, simulations.

00:43:52.240 --> 00:43:56.529
You are processing it, generating data, processing
data, and analyzing data.

00:43:56.529 --> 00:44:00.019
And then if you get into data interpretation
and so forth, where you're going to interpret

00:44:00.019 --> 00:44:04.270
the results, come back, do visualization,
and so forth.

00:44:04.270 --> 00:44:09.770
And so going back to our cooking analogy,
we sort of had the shopping stage, the preparing

00:44:09.770 --> 00:44:12.990
stage, the cooking stage, and the storing
stage.

00:44:12.990 --> 00:44:18.940
And so questions that one wants to ask is
definitely where and how do you get the data?

00:44:18.940 --> 00:44:21.299
What format is the data in?

00:44:21.299 --> 00:44:25.800
How can you actually integrate or subset data
sets?

00:44:25.800 --> 00:44:28.039
And then, of course, how you analyze the data.

00:44:28.039 --> 00:44:29.290
What is the analysis function?

00:44:29.290 --> 00:44:32.809
What are the different parameters that are
needed to customize each step?

00:44:32.809 --> 00:44:33.809
Et cetera.

00:44:33.809 --> 00:44:42.550
And I will say that when you have an end to
end-- when you have a complex workflow or

00:44:42.550 --> 00:44:46.880
experiment, it's challenging to do this.

00:44:46.880 --> 00:44:50.069
And it requires a lot of time and a lot of
thought, but ultimately, these are the kind

00:44:50.069 --> 00:44:54.480
of things that will enable truly reproducible
science.

00:44:54.480 --> 00:44:57.710
And I think one of the great things about
workflows is that once you do this work of

00:44:57.710 --> 00:45:04.730
really analyzing and this detail and putting
things together and standardizing the process,

00:45:04.730 --> 00:45:08.650
the data and the process, you then have a
tool.

00:45:08.650 --> 00:45:18.750
And that also becomes-- that sort of conceptualization
of the data itself is actually very valuable.

00:45:18.750 --> 00:45:23.079
In terms of the steps for searching for the
data, of course, you need to know what you're

00:45:23.079 --> 00:45:30.369
looking for at first, so different data formats,
and the meaning of the data, how it actually

00:45:30.369 --> 00:45:34.769
scientifically and conceptually relates to
the analysis that you're doing.

00:45:34.769 --> 00:45:40.650
And then it helps if you, of course, for you
to know exactly where you need to find that

00:45:40.650 --> 00:45:43.150
data, so where to find what you're looking
for.

00:45:43.150 --> 00:45:49.500
And here, I'm sure there will be other talks
in the series, where some of these data sources

00:45:49.500 --> 00:45:51.460
are discussed in more detail.

00:45:51.460 --> 00:45:54.859
But there's many surgical data archives and
repositories, and people are working very

00:45:54.859 --> 00:46:00.010
hard to make sure these are standardized with
correct data ontologies and metadata standards

00:46:00.010 --> 00:46:01.820
and so forth.

00:46:01.820 --> 00:46:05.759
And anyway, the data could also, for example,
be coming from your own sensor networks or

00:46:05.759 --> 00:46:10.190
other scientific instruments, even just in
your own lab.

00:46:10.190 --> 00:46:15.609
Another important component that needs to
be considered in general in the scientific

00:46:15.609 --> 00:46:18.470
enterprise, but also with workflows, is do
you have to move data?

00:46:18.470 --> 00:46:22.260
Sometimes you do need to move data, and then
depending on how big it is, that could be

00:46:22.260 --> 00:46:23.260
a problem.

00:46:23.260 --> 00:46:26.650
And maybe you need to come up with different
ways to access the data so that you don't

00:46:26.650 --> 00:46:28.130
have to move it.

00:46:28.130 --> 00:46:32.560
But in any case, it's just to note that moving
data can sometimes, of course, be a big challenge,

00:46:32.560 --> 00:46:36.670
especially very large data sets, and it's
something to think about and to try to avoid,

00:46:36.670 --> 00:46:39.340
I would say, at all costs if you can help
it.

00:46:39.340 --> 00:46:46.240
So there's various different access technologies
also that can be used if data moving is needed.

00:46:46.240 --> 00:46:55.079
Again, I guess I sort of already covered this,
but we have the ability-- one of the great

00:46:55.079 --> 00:47:00.920
things about developing these workflows is
that once you have them developed, we say

00:47:00.920 --> 00:47:06.900
Kepler-- it's actually not that difficult
to change where you are executing the jobs,

00:47:06.900 --> 00:47:12.180
the data generation jobs with the analysis
and processing.

00:47:12.180 --> 00:47:16.859
And so you can move around to different computing
resources depending on the requirements of

00:47:16.859 --> 00:47:17.980
your job.

00:47:17.980 --> 00:47:21.869
And for example, some steps may be more memory
intensive.

00:47:21.869 --> 00:47:23.190
Some may be more compute intensive.

00:47:23.190 --> 00:47:25.570
Some may port very well to GPUs.

00:47:25.570 --> 00:47:29.400
You can be flexible about that, and you should
be so that you can really gainfully take advantage

00:47:29.400 --> 00:47:36.440
of as much-- so you can get as much advantage
as you can of the different resources.

00:47:36.440 --> 00:47:43.940
And then, as I mentioned, once you do the
work of building the workflow, you build it

00:47:43.940 --> 00:47:45.800
once, and it is a lot of work to build it.

00:47:45.800 --> 00:47:50.910
But then you can run it many times, and you
have terrific possibilities for reuse.

00:47:50.910 --> 00:48:01.119
So hopefully, the same workflow will support
your computational experimental work and dynamic

00:48:01.119 --> 00:48:03.690
scalability on many different platforms.

00:48:03.690 --> 00:48:09.109
And the scalability can be based on a number
of different data features like data volume

00:48:09.109 --> 00:48:10.109
and velocity.

00:48:10.109 --> 00:48:14.750
This could change depending on those aspects
of it.

00:48:14.750 --> 00:48:19.779
Maybe you might need to change something about
the modeling based on different optimization

00:48:19.779 --> 00:48:25.529
criteria, and then also, of course, these
workflows, once you build them, you can run

00:48:25.529 --> 00:48:30.559
them many times, but you can also alter it
based on changes in network storage and computing

00:48:30.559 --> 00:48:31.559
availability.

00:48:31.559 --> 00:48:33.390
And so these are tweaks that can be made.

00:48:33.390 --> 00:48:38.619
And then one of my favorite parts really of
the workflow, I have to say, is the saving

00:48:38.619 --> 00:48:43.070
and sharing of the reports and final products
with your team and collaborators and with

00:48:43.070 --> 00:48:44.750
the broader community.

00:48:44.750 --> 00:48:52.059
So computational data science is right in
the middle of bridging the gap between the

00:48:52.059 --> 00:48:58.829
data that is generated and really the key
scientific insight.

00:48:58.829 --> 00:49:07.029
And so bringing everything together in a workflow
and in a scalable automated workflow like

00:49:07.029 --> 00:49:12.829
I showed you with Kepler
is something that can be tremendously powerful.

00:49:12.829 --> 00:49:19.769
And so I think another sort of aspect of this
is that people should, they can and they should

00:49:19.769 --> 00:49:25.740
use whatever favorite tools or programs you
need to answer the question to implement the

00:49:25.740 --> 00:49:27.130
methods in your workflow.

00:49:27.130 --> 00:49:32.099
There's no reason to feel that you need to
be limited.

00:49:32.099 --> 00:49:36.849
I think really the possibilities are almost
endless in terms of different possibilities

00:49:36.849 --> 00:49:41.619
to enumerate within your own scientific domain.

00:49:41.619 --> 00:49:47.499
And then the great thing is that the process
itself is shareable, so you have-- when you

00:49:47.499 --> 00:49:53.430
build these workflows, they themselves are
their own sort of data structure.

00:49:53.430 --> 00:49:55.630
And you can exchange them.

00:49:55.630 --> 00:50:00.059
Communities, of course, are working very hard
to standardize them.

00:50:00.059 --> 00:50:03.710
Again, as I've mentioned multiple times, they're
scalable.

00:50:03.710 --> 00:50:10.430
And it helps-these workflows also help enable
really sort of accountable science.

00:50:10.430 --> 00:50:19.390
So again, sort of not to beat a dead horse,
I guess is what they would say, but these

00:50:19.390 --> 00:50:24.750
scientific experiments involve many different
kinds of data coming from different sources,

00:50:24.750 --> 00:50:25.950
and there's different versions.

00:50:25.950 --> 00:50:30.990
This is the other thing that has driven us
crazy is how you can run a particular program--

00:50:30.990 --> 00:50:34.720
for example, docking program one day on one
particular architecture, and then there's

00:50:34.720 --> 00:50:38.730
some update that's made, and now it's version
5.7 instead of 4.2.

00:50:38.730 --> 00:50:43.570
And maybe it didn't break any of the scripts
that you used as input, but maybe it changed

00:50:43.570 --> 00:50:45.440
the results because of something.

00:50:45.440 --> 00:50:53.299
And it's possible sometimes that trainees
and researchers don't necessarily know the

00:50:53.299 --> 00:50:58.450
fine, fine detail of what's going on, especially
for people who are domain scientists and who

00:50:58.450 --> 00:51:07.670
are pulling together 20 different tools in
one biomedical experiment.

00:51:07.670 --> 00:51:12.410
And so one of the great things about these
workflows is that a lot of that versioning

00:51:12.410 --> 00:51:19.589
and dependencies and everything, it can be
recorded through something called provenance

00:51:19.589 --> 00:51:26.910
and tracked and then reported and stored so
that people really know exactly what was happening

00:51:26.910 --> 00:51:30.119
at what given time, and you can refollow that
data flow.

00:51:30.119 --> 00:51:36.319
And, of course, so that's just on the data
and the execution side, but you can really

00:51:36.319 --> 00:51:42.829
very clearly see and document what processes
ran in which order, which steps actually depended

00:51:42.829 --> 00:51:48.559
on the output from the previous step, which
could just run in parallel separately, which

00:51:48.559 --> 00:51:53.250
libraries were used, and also who generated
what.

00:51:53.250 --> 00:51:59.020
So if you have collaborators, it's a very
good way to sort of share everything.

00:51:59.020 --> 00:52:03.690
And so provenance is something that I know
has been talked about already more technically

00:52:03.690 --> 00:52:06.029
for sure in the BD2K series.

00:52:06.029 --> 00:52:11.670
But basically, for those who haven't heard
it, provenance basically is something that

00:52:11.670 --> 00:52:16.910
helps tremendously with what we call research
accountability and reproducibility.

00:52:16.910 --> 00:52:21.089
It's basically, according to Wikipedia that
would define it as the chronology of ownership,

00:52:21.089 --> 00:52:24.349
custody, or location of the historical object.

00:52:24.349 --> 00:52:31.960
So when we think about provenance in terms
of workflows, this is really sort of like

00:52:31.960 --> 00:52:35.280
digital notebook, but on steroids.

00:52:35.280 --> 00:52:42.660
It actually can contain different information
depending on what you said, so it can be configured

00:52:42.660 --> 00:52:47.130
to retain particular aspects of the experiment
that you think are important, but it can capture

00:52:47.130 --> 00:52:49.432
everything-- inputs, outputs,
particular aspects of the experiment that

00:52:49.432 --> 00:52:51.160
you think are important, but it can capture
everything-- inputs, outputs, intermediate

00:52:51.160 --> 00:52:55.569
results, all the work, the parameters and
details of the different components of the

00:52:55.569 --> 00:52:59.819
workflows, so all those different actors or
boxes, which I showed you, all the steps,

00:52:59.819 --> 00:53:02.670
how the steps are linked together, all the
different parameters.

00:53:02.670 --> 00:53:12.270
This can actually be recorded in with data
provenance or with these provenance elements.

00:53:12.270 --> 00:53:14.619
And this is useful, because it keeps the association.

00:53:14.619 --> 00:53:19.999
It retains the association of results of the
particular workflow to processes.

00:53:19.999 --> 00:53:24.339
It also makes it easier to validate and regenerate
results and processes, because you know exactly

00:53:24.339 --> 00:53:27.029
how you got them in the first place.

00:53:27.029 --> 00:53:32.640
It's much more easy-- it's much easier to
compare different workflow versions, do parameter

00:53:32.640 --> 00:53:38.220
optimizations, et cetera, and sometimes some
of these programs actually allow you to do

00:53:38.220 --> 00:53:42.589
sort of intelligent or facilitated reruns
and failure recovery.

00:53:42.589 --> 00:53:46.339
So different questions you can ask with provenance
or different questions that are answered with

00:53:46.339 --> 00:53:50.150
provenance, for example-- you can find out
how long did this particular workflow run

00:53:50.150 --> 00:53:51.279
take.

00:53:51.279 --> 00:53:54.089
What parameter values were used?

00:53:54.089 --> 00:53:59.369
What different components or processes or
actors does the workflow have?

00:53:59.369 --> 00:54:02.180
How long does one particular actor take?

00:54:02.180 --> 00:54:03.749
So how long is one step?

00:54:03.749 --> 00:54:05.789
Where's my bottleneck?

00:54:05.789 --> 00:54:08.980
And what workflows did a specific user run
on a given date?

00:54:08.980 --> 00:54:11.910
These are all questions that one can dig in
on.

00:54:11.910 --> 00:54:15.740
And so again, there's many reasons why you
might want to share workflows.

00:54:15.740 --> 00:54:20.050
For example, if you're doing collaborative
research, if you want to just inform people

00:54:20.050 --> 00:54:23.859
of progress or formalize application development,
et cetera.

00:54:23.859 --> 00:54:30.180
And also for publications, I think one of
the also movements that we're trying to catalyze,

00:54:30.180 --> 00:54:38.549
at least in the molecular dynamics community,
which is challenged with-- suffers like many

00:54:38.549 --> 00:54:39.960
areas with reproducibility.

00:54:39.960 --> 00:54:47.789
We can actually publish-- along with our scientific
paper, we can publish these workflow protocols.

00:54:47.789 --> 00:54:52.730
And hopefully, we're trying to develop a community
of researchers, who are developing these.

00:54:52.730 --> 00:54:59.520
And so we can all be ensured that we are really
performing research in a transparent reproducible

00:54:59.520 --> 00:55:04.829
way for the maximal gain for the community.

00:55:04.829 --> 00:55:13.140
And so when you're sharing workflow results,
there's different ways that you can share

00:55:13.140 --> 00:55:14.140
workflows.

00:55:14.140 --> 00:55:18.859
With versioning, for example, you can use
GitHub to do that.

00:55:18.859 --> 00:55:23.609
There's also cloud file hosting services,
so you can share workflows using Dropbox,

00:55:23.609 --> 00:55:25.299
Google Drive, et cetera.

00:55:25.299 --> 00:55:31.119
And then the other thing that we also do,
and I think a lot of others have taken to

00:55:31.119 --> 00:55:36.650
sort of recently is to create iPython notebooks
that share execution history, input parameters,

00:55:36.650 --> 00:55:37.650
and results.

00:55:37.650 --> 00:55:43.710
So there's many different ways that workflows
can come into the enterprise, and certainly,

00:55:43.710 --> 00:55:46.270
sharing of the results is super important.

00:55:46.270 --> 00:55:51.050
So I only have five minutes, and I want to
at least leave a couple minutes for questions

00:55:51.050 --> 00:55:52.050
in case there are any.

00:55:52.050 --> 00:55:58.060
But before I leave, I wanted to say I know
this was a very high level introduction to

00:55:58.060 --> 00:56:02.190
workflows and pipelines for biomedical researchers.

00:56:02.190 --> 00:56:05.519
So if you want to get more information, of
course, Google is your friend.

00:56:05.519 --> 00:56:10.710
But I'll just give you a couple of leads,
so one place where you can get some hands-on

00:56:10.710 --> 00:56:14.210
experience, you can go to biobigdata.ucsd.edu.

00:56:14.210 --> 00:56:26.039
There's a scalable-- so there is a Kepler
class or course there is-- it should be very

00:56:26.039 --> 00:56:27.749
easy for people to run.

00:56:27.749 --> 00:56:32.349
It comes with a virtual machine that you can
have everything, all the data and everything

00:56:32.349 --> 00:56:35.079
you need installed and lectures.

00:56:35.079 --> 00:56:42.660
You can actually go to this site and retrieve
that information and actually go through the

00:56:42.660 --> 00:56:43.660
course.

00:56:43.660 --> 00:56:49.380
And if you want to see-- the first course
is just on the Kepler software framework.

00:56:49.380 --> 00:56:55.819
If you want to see an actual application of
how workflows can come into biomedical research,

00:56:55.819 --> 00:57:02.779
we also have a class that we call Data to
Structural Models that walks through image

00:57:02.779 --> 00:57:07.079
analysis and segmentation for electron tomography.

00:57:07.079 --> 00:57:14.289
And it applies a Kepler workflow for image
segmentation and analysis.

00:57:14.289 --> 00:57:18.329
And so you can get in a sort of more domain-specific
example there.

00:57:18.329 --> 00:57:25.410
And of course, also, I need to give a shout
out to ERuDIte and the BD2K training coordinating

00:57:25.410 --> 00:57:26.410
center.

00:57:26.410 --> 00:57:31.509
They also-- I just very quickly searched for
workflows in ERuDIte, and you can see that

00:57:31.509 --> 00:57:37.289
there's 22 different results and a number
of different people who will show and talk

00:57:37.289 --> 00:57:43.519
about examples that highlight workflows in
biomedical research.

00:57:43.519 --> 00:57:45.079
And I do have an acknowledgement slide.

00:57:45.079 --> 00:57:51.950
Of course, I just want to thank the BD2K efforts,
the NIH generally, my groups.

00:57:51.950 --> 00:58:00.400
Ilkay Altintas actually is my collaborator,
co-PI on the BD2K R25 award for the biomedical

00:58:00.400 --> 00:58:07.710
big data training collaborative, and she's
a major developer of Kepler and has been a

00:58:07.710 --> 00:58:12.029
terrific resource for information on workflows
and pipelines.

00:58:12.029 --> 00:58:14.180
So I think I have like two minutes.

00:58:14.180 --> 00:58:16.420
I would love to take questions if there's
any questions.

00:58:16.420 --> 00:58:18.040
Thank you.

00:58:18.040 --> 00:58:21.049
That was a fabulous talk.

00:58:21.049 --> 00:58:22.569
There are a few questions.

00:58:22.569 --> 00:58:23.569
Great.

00:58:23.569 --> 00:58:25.609
I'll combine a couple of them.

00:58:25.609 --> 00:58:34.900
This one is about sharing of data, and you
mentioned a couple of different options-FTP

00:58:34.900 --> 00:58:37.710
and [INAUDIBLE].

00:58:37.710 --> 00:58:39.029
Are there any others?

00:58:39.029 --> 00:58:42.039
Does internet too improve the transfer rates?

00:58:42.039 --> 00:58:45.770
Is there any advice you can give about that?

00:58:45.770 --> 00:58:49.080
Speak to-- yes.

00:58:49.080 --> 00:58:53.640
I will say that in general, we try to avoid
moving data, but of course, we do have to

00:58:53.640 --> 00:58:54.640
move some.

00:58:54.640 --> 00:58:55.680
And that's a very good question.

00:58:55.680 --> 00:59:04.920
I know that there are-- other than the examples
that I showed, this is a tough one for me

00:59:04.920 --> 00:59:06.950
to answer.

00:59:06.950 --> 00:59:08.660
Yeah.

00:59:08.660 --> 00:59:11.420
We generally try to avoid moving huge data
sets.

00:59:11.420 --> 00:59:14.000
When we do, we just use sort of like the normal
protocols.

00:59:14.000 --> 00:59:18.710
But definitely, I'm sure that there are many
people who are working on more effective and

00:59:18.710 --> 00:59:21.030
efficient ways to actually transfer data.

00:59:21.030 --> 00:59:24.420
One thing that we've done too to try to avoid
transferring data, of course, is to try to

00:59:24.420 --> 00:59:27.319
work on other technologies like remote visualization
and so forth.

00:59:27.319 --> 00:59:33.829
That's sort of been one of our major efforts.

00:59:33.829 --> 00:59:36.300
So thank you.

00:59:36.300 --> 00:59:45.890
I think moving the computation and the visualization
to the data is what is more common now or

00:59:45.890 --> 00:59:51.019
what will be even more common in the future.

00:59:51.019 --> 00:59:53.279
So one more question.

00:59:53.279 --> 00:59:57.920
What do you think about the common workflow
language, and does Kepler support CWL?

00:59:57.920 --> 01:00:01.180
That's another very good question.

01:00:01.180 --> 01:00:03.200
I wish Ilkay was here to answer that.

01:00:03.200 --> 01:00:04.509
I'm pretty sure it does.

01:00:04.509 --> 01:00:08.819
I think common-- I generally think common
workflow language is going to be a good move

01:00:08.819 --> 01:00:09.819
for the community.

01:00:09.819 --> 01:00:12.900
I don't know when we have actually the hard
stop here.

01:00:12.900 --> 01:00:19.400
I do believe it does support it, but I'm not
sure exactly to what extent it does.

01:00:19.400 --> 01:00:25.859
I do think we need to stop now, but thank
you very much, Rommie.

01:00:25.859 --> 01:00:30.359
This was a wonderful talk, and we really appreciate
you coming here and sharing your expertise

01:00:30.359 --> 01:00:31.359
with us.

01:00:31.359 --> 01:00:32.359
It was fun.

01:00:32.359 --> 01:00:33.359
Thanks.

01:00:33.359 --> 01:00:34.359
OK.

01:00:34.359 --> 01:00:35.359
Goodbye.

01:00:35.359 --> 01:00:36.359
Thank you all.

01:00:36.359 --> 01:00:36.470
